            <ArticleTitle>The epidemiology of headache and migraine, and its meaning for neurological services.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>128-35</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Estimates of the incidence and prevalence of migraine and tension-type headache are strongly dependent upon the diagnostic criteria employed. The inter-rater reliability of classification of different types of headache is poor. Whatever the &quot;true&quot; prevalence of headache and migraine, the demand for neurological services is culturally determined. A small alteration in tolerance of a population's headaches may lead to a demand for a greatly increased number of neurologists.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
